 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 70,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "RAF BRIZE NORTON. The RAF's biggest station and the home of the Airbridge. Flights to the territories leave from the terminal: follow the road south. NORTH: the Cotswolds. Please do not feed the planespotters."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "brize_church",
   "title": "St Britius Church",
   "text": "The village church is dedicated to St Britius, or Brice, a fifth-century bishop of Tours, and has Norman origins. Its name is thought to have given the village its name, 'Brize' being a worn-down 'Brice'. Down the road, the airfield took the village's name when it opened in 1937."
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 7,
   "text": "BRIZE NORTON VILLAGE. Please drive slowly: the geese have the right of way, and so have the Hercules."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 7,
   "plaque": "brize_village",
   "title": "Brize Norton Village",
   "text": "Brize Norton is an Oxfordshire village of Cotswold-stone cottages that happens to have the largest station of the Royal Air Force over the hedge. The RAF opened the airfield in 1937. The village has learnt to stop and look up whenever a big grey aircraft goes over, and it goes over often."
  },
  {
   "x": 76,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "brize_est",
   "title": "Station Brize Norton",
   "text": "RAF Brize Norton is the largest station in the Royal Air Force. It is home to the RAF's air transport and air-to-air refuelling fleets, which means that when British troops, aid or post need to go a very long way, they usually start here. Its runway is just over 3 kilometres long, and the air stays remarkably busy."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "brize_voyager",
   "title": "The Voyager",
   "text": "The Airbus Voyager is the RAF's tanker and transport: a modified A330 airliner that can carry passengers and cargo as well as refuel fighters in mid-air from underwing hoses. It entered RAF service in 2011, and it flies the Airbridge to the South Atlantic. Some of the Voyagers were painted in a livery with the Union flag on the tail; one carried the Royal Family."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 42,
   "plaque": "brize_atlas",
   "title": "The Atlas",
   "text": "The Airbus A400M Atlas is the RAF's newer transport, with four propeller engines that look old-fashioned and do the job extremely well. The RAF took its first one in 2014, and it can land on short rough strips where a jet airliner cannot. Brize Norton also looks after its crews."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 47,
   "plaque": "brize_vc10",
   "title": "The VC10 Years",
   "text": "The Vickers VC10 was a British jet airliner built in the 1960s with four engines on the tail, and the RAF flew it from Brize Norton from 1966 as a transport and, later, a tanker. It was loud, fast and loved. The last RAF VC10 flew in 2013, and several of the airframes are kept in museums."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "brize_sac",
   "title": "The Americans at Brize",
   "text": "In the early 1950s the United States Air Force's Strategic Air Command took over Brize Norton as a base for its bombers, and for years there were more American voices in the Carterton pubs than English ones. The station was handed back to the RAF in 1965, which is when it became the Transport Command base it is today."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "brize_horsa",
   "title": "The Gliders",
   "text": "In the Second World War Brize Norton trained glider pilots and paratroopers. The big plywood Airspeed Horsa glider was built in thousands and carried troops and equipment into battle at Normandy and Arnhem, towed by bombers, landing with no engine. A Horsa is on show in the heritage hangar's collection of old aircraft and older stories."
  },
  {
   "x": 74,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "brize_airbridge",
   "title": "The South Atlantic Airbridge",
   "text": "After the Falklands conflict of 1982 the RAF began a regular air service between Brize Norton and the islands, with a stop at Ascension Island on the way. It is called the South Atlantic Airbridge, and it covers about 13,000 kilometres each way, which is a very long way to take a Christmas card."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 47,
   "plaque": "brize_runway",
   "title": "The Runway",
   "text": "The main runway at Brize Norton is a little over 3 kilometres long, long enough for the heaviest aircraft the RAF flies. The apron is a great concrete car park for aeroplanes, and the lights down the runway used to be lit by hand. Today the Control Tower looks after them."
  }
 ],
